package cosmonic:agent@0.4.1;

/// An agent may be handed runs. Exported by an agent and called by whatever
/// drives it: a host (Cosmonic Desktop's daemon; a Cosmonic Control trigger
/// that starts a run from an HTTP request or a message), the implementer of
/// `spawn` for a spawned child, or a component that adapts another protocol
/// onto it. Named after `wasi:cli/run`. Not published; for discussion.
///
/// The agent needs no ingress: nothing but its driver can reach it. The
/// session it works in is not named here. It is the agent's
/// `cosmonic:agent/session` import, which the host binds to the workload, so
/// a driver cannot point a run at a session by naming one.
///
/// Journal. A run keeps the session conventions in `session`: the agent
/// writes the person's message and every step as `agent.step.v1` entries, and
/// each model or tool call between `agent.op.v1` and `agent.op-result.v1`.
/// It also writes `agent.run.v1` (`run`: the run id) in the commit that opens
/// the run, with the person's message, and `agent.run-result.v1` (`run`, and
/// `outcome`: done, failed or cancelled, with `finish` or the error case) in
/// the commit that ends it. The events a run streams are a live view of what
/// it commits; the journal is the record, and a reopened conversation is
/// drawn from it.
@unstable(feature = agent-run)
interface run {
    @unstable(feature = agent-run)
    use cosmonic:agent/inference-types@0.4.0.{user-message};
    @unstable(feature = agent-run)
    use run-types.{run-options, started-run, start-error};

    /// Start one run: take the message and work until the agent replies, a
    /// limit in `options` ends it, it fails, or the driver cancels it.
    ///
    /// Resolves once the run is accepted, not when it ends: the agent has
    /// committed the person's message and `agent.run.v1` to the session, so a
    /// run that fails or is cancelled later still shows what was asked.
    /// Returns `start-error` without starting and without writing: `busy`
    /// while a run is in progress (and after a cancellation, until that run
    /// has actually stopped, so two runs never overlap), `duplicate` for a
    /// `run-id` already started, `invalid-input` for a message or options it
    /// cannot take.
    ///
    /// Once started, the agent streams events as it works and resolves the
    /// outcome after its last commit, so a driver that redraws from the
    /// journal when the outcome arrives finds the run recorded. Dropping the
    /// events stream requests cancellation: the agent stops its model and tool
    /// calls, records each one as the session conventions say (cancelled, or
    /// unresolved when its effects are unknown), and resolves the outcome to
    /// `cancelled` only once nothing of the run is still running. Dropping
    /// the outcome future as well stops listening; the agent still winds the
    /// run down and records it.
    @unstable(feature = agent-run)
    run: async func(
        message: user-message,
        options: run-options,
    ) -> result<started-run, start-error>;
}
